1
00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:07.750
[S01] The Uniform Code of Military Justice specifies court martial for any officer

2
00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:11.830
[S01] who sends a soldier into battle without a weapon.

3
00:00:11.830 --> 00:00:15.509
[S01] There ought to be a similar protection for students because students shouldn't go out

4
00:00:15.509 --> 00:00:18.320
[S01] into life without an ability to communicate.

5
00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:23.480
[S01] And that's because your success in life will be determined largely by your ability to

6
00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:31.989
[S01] speak, your ability to write, and the quality of your ideas, in that order.

7
00:00:31.989 --> 00:00:39.039
[S01] I know that I can be successful in this because the quality of communication, your speaking,

8
00:00:39.039 --> 00:00:42.460
[S01] your writing, is largely determined by this formula.

9
00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:48.850
[S01] It's a matter of how much knowledge you have, how much you practice with that knowledge,

10
00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:50.869
[S01] and your inherent talent.

11
00:00:50.869 --> 00:00:53.420
[S01] And notice that the T is very small.

12
00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:57.140
[S01] What really matters is what you know.

13
00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:03.890
[S01] This point came to me suddenly a few decades ago when I was skiing at Sun Valley.

14
00:01:03.890 --> 00:01:12.180
[S01] I had heard that it was celebrity weekend, and one of the celebrities was Mary Lou Breton,

15
00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:16.680
[S01] famous Olympic gymnast, perfect tens in the vault.

16
00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:21.519
[S01] And I heard that she was a novice at skiing, so when the opportune moment arrived, I looked

17
00:01:21.519 --> 00:01:27.930
[S01] over on the novice slope and saw this young woman who, when she became unbalanced, went

18
00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:29.609
[S01] like that.

19
00:01:29.609 --> 00:01:31.540
[S01] And I said, "That's got to be her.

20
00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:34.859
[S01] That must be the gymnast."

21
00:01:34.859 --> 00:01:39.609
[S01] But then it occurred to me, "I'm a much better skier than she is, and she's an Olympic athlete,

22
00:01:39.609 --> 00:01:44.420
[S01] not only an ordinary Olympic athlete, an outstanding one."

23
00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:50.250
[S01] And I was a better skier because I had the K, and I had the P, and all she had was the

24
00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:51.810
[S01] T.

25
00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:56.420
[S01] So you can get a lot better than people who may have inherent talents if you have the

26
00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:58.609
[S01] right amount of knowledge.

27
00:01:58.609 --> 00:02:02.140
[S01] So that's what my objective is today, and here's my promise.

28
00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:09.560
[S01] Today you will see some examples of what you can put in your armamentarium of speaking

29
00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:11.719
[S01] techniques.

30
00:02:11.719 --> 00:02:18.840
[S01] And it will be the case that some one of those examples, some heuristic, some technique,

31
00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:24.120
[S01] maybe only one, will be the one that gets you the job.

32
00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:26.719
[S01] And so this is a very nonlinear process.

33
00:02:26.719 --> 00:02:28.969
[S01] You never know when it's going to happen.

34
00:02:28.969 --> 00:02:34.509
[S01] But that is my promise, by the end of the next 60 minutes, you'll have been exposed

35
00:02:34.509 --> 00:02:39.469
[S01] to a lot of ideas, some of which you'll incorporate into your own repertoire, and

36
00:02:39.469 --> 00:02:50.090
[S01] they will ensure that you get the maximum opportunity to have your ideas valued and

37
00:02:50.090 --> 00:02:52.620
[S01] accepted by the people you speak with.

38
00:02:52.620 --> 00:03:02.180
[S01] Now, in order to do that, we have to have a rule of engagement, and that is no laptops,

39
00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:03.180
[S01] no cell phones.

40
00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:08.620
[S01] So if you could close those, I'll start up as soon as you're done.

41
00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:16.340
[S01] Some people ask why that is a rule of engagement, and the answer is, we humans only have one

42
00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:17.620
[S01] language processor.

43
00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:20.060
[S01] And if your language processor is engaged...

44
00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:21.810
[S01] Could you shut the laptop, please?

45
00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:29.229
[S01] If your language processor is engaged, browsing the web or reading your email, you're distracted.

46
00:03:29.229 --> 00:03:32.150
[S01] And worse yet, you distract all of the people around you.

47
00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:33.789
[S01] Studies have shown that.

48
00:03:33.789 --> 00:03:41.509
[S01] And worse yet, if I see an open laptop somewhere back there or up here, it drives me nuts,

49
00:03:41.509 --> 00:03:43.590
[S01] and I do a worse job.

50
00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:49.680
[S01] And so that ensures that all of your friends who are paying attention don't get the performance

51
00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:52.340
[S01] that they came to have.

52
00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:55.039
[S01] So, that's it for preamble.

53
00:03:55.039 --> 00:03:57.870
[S01] Let's get started.

54
00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:06.840
[S01] First thing to talk about, of course, is how to start.

55
00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:20.730
[S01] Some people think the right thing to do is to start a talk with a joke.

56
00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:22.829
[S01] I don't recommend it.

57
00:04:22.829 --> 00:04:29.579
[S01] And the reason is that in the beginning of a talk, people are still putting their laptops

58
00:04:29.579 --> 00:04:38.019
[S01] away, they're becoming adjusted to your speaking parameters, to your vocal parameters,

59
00:04:38.019 --> 00:04:39.879
[S01] and they're not ready for a joke.

60
00:04:39.879 --> 00:04:41.220
[S01] So it doesn't work very well.

61
00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:43.720
[S01] They usually fall flat.

62
00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:51.220
[S01] What you want to do instead is start with empowerment promise.

63
00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:55.110
[S01] You want to tell people what they're going to know at the end of the hour that they

64
00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:57.660
[S01] didn't know at the beginning of the hour.

65
00:04:57.660 --> 00:04:58.949
[S01] It's an empowerment promise.

66
00:04:58.949 --> 00:05:00.329
[S01] It's the reason for being here.

67
00:05:02.649 --> 00:05:04.060
[S02] What would be an example?

68
00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:05.370
[S02] Oh, I see.

69
00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:09.310
[S02] At the end of this 60 minutes,

70
00:05:09.310 --> 00:05:12.230
[S02] you will know things about speaking you don't know now,

71
00:05:12.230 --> 00:05:13.879
[S02] and something among those things you know

72
00:05:13.879 --> 00:05:15.480
[S02] will make a difference in your life.

73
00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:17.370
[S02] Yeah, that's an empowerment promise.

74
00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:20.139
[S02] So that's the best way to start.

75
00:05:20.139 --> 00:05:22.759
[S02] So now that I've talked a little bit about how to start,

76
00:05:22.759 --> 00:05:25.360
[S02] what I want to do is give you some samples

77
00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:30.149
[S02] of heuristics that are always on my mind when I give a talk.

78
00:05:30.149 --> 00:05:32.529
[S02] And first of these heuristics

79
00:05:32.529 --> 00:05:37.680
[S02] is that it's a good idea to cycle on the subject.

80
00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:42.430
[S02] Go around it, go around it again, go around it again.

81
00:05:42.430 --> 00:05:47.120
[S02] Some people say, "Tell them what you want to tell them.

82
00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:49.889
[S02] Tell them again, and then tell them a third time."

83
00:05:49.889 --> 00:05:53.110
[S02] As if people weren't intelligent.

84
00:05:53.110 --> 00:05:56.209
[S02] But the point is, the reason is, well,

85
00:05:56.209 --> 00:05:56.930
[S02] there are many reasons.

86
00:05:56.930 --> 00:05:59.430
[S02] One of which is, at any given moment,

87
00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:01.379
[S02] about 20% of you will be fogged out,

88
00:06:01.379 --> 00:06:03.310
[S02] no matter what the lecture is.

89
00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:05.029
[S02] So if you want to ensure that the probability

90
00:06:05.029 --> 00:06:06.980
[S02] that everybody gets it is high,

91
00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:10.310
[S02] you need to say it three times.

92
00:06:10.310 --> 00:06:11.589
[S02] So cycling is one of the things

93
00:06:11.589 --> 00:06:14.839
[S02] that I always think about when I give a talk.

94
00:06:14.839 --> 00:06:17.139
[S02] Another thing I think about is,

95
00:06:17.139 --> 00:06:19.250
[S02] in explaining my idea,

96
00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:25.750
[S02] I want to build a fence around it

97
00:06:25.750 --> 00:06:29.110
[S02] so that it's not confused with somebody else's idea.

98
00:06:29.110 --> 00:06:31.290
[S02] So if you were from Mars

99
00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:33.990
[S02] and I was teaching you about what an arch is,

100
00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:36.680
[S02] I might say to you, "Well, that's an arch."

101
00:06:36.680 --> 00:06:38.720
[S02] And that's not to be confused with some other things

102
00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:40.170
[S02] that other people might think is an arch.

103
00:06:40.170 --> 00:06:43.810
[S02] This is not an arch.

104
00:06:43.810 --> 00:06:45.170
[S02] That's not an arch.

105
00:06:45.170 --> 00:06:47.009
[S02] I'm building a fence around my idea

106
00:06:47.009 --> 00:06:48.209
[S02] so that it can be distinguished

107
00:06:48.209 --> 00:06:50.220
[S02] from somebody else's idea.

108
00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:52.579
[S02] So in a more technical sense, I might say,

109
00:06:52.579 --> 00:06:55.920
[S02] "Well, my algorithm might seem similar

110
00:06:55.920 --> 00:06:56.920
[S02] to Jones's algorithm,

111
00:06:56.920 --> 00:06:59.540
[S02] except his is exponential and mine's linear."

112
00:06:59.540 --> 00:07:01.110
[S02] That's putting a fence around your idea

113
00:07:01.110 --> 00:07:03.560
[S02] so that people can not be confused

114
00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:08.160
[S02] about how it might relate to something else.

115
00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:11.410
[S02] The third thing on this list of samples

116
00:07:11.410 --> 00:07:25.879
[S02] is the idea of verbal punctuation.

117
00:07:25.879 --> 00:07:28.540
[S02] And the idea here is that

118
00:07:28.540 --> 00:07:31.779
[S02] because people will occasionally fog out

119
00:07:31.779 --> 00:07:33.910
[S02] and need to get back on the bus,

120
00:07:33.910 --> 00:07:36.560
[S02] you need to provide some landmark places

121
00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:38.879
[S02] where you're announcing

122
00:07:38.879 --> 00:07:41.439
[S02] that it's a good time to get back on.

123
00:07:41.439 --> 00:07:43.129
[S02] So I might, in this talk,

124
00:07:43.129 --> 00:07:47.009
[S02] say something about this being my outline.

125
00:07:47.009 --> 00:07:47.870
[S02] The first thing we're going to do

126
00:07:47.870 --> 00:07:50.139
[S02] is talk about how to start.

127
00:07:50.139 --> 00:07:52.750
[S02] Then we're going to deal with these four samples.

128
00:07:52.750 --> 00:07:54.939
[S02] And among these four samples,

129
00:07:54.939 --> 00:07:57.759
[S02] I've talked about the first idea, that's cycling.

130
00:07:57.759 --> 00:07:59.009
[S02] The second idea, building a fence.

131
00:07:59.009 --> 00:08:03.230
[S02] And now the third idea is verbal punctuation.

132
00:08:03.230 --> 00:08:05.949
[S02] So I'm enumerating, I'm providing numbers,

133
00:08:05.949 --> 00:08:07.449
[S02] I'm giving you a sense

134
00:08:07.449 --> 00:08:09.230
[S02] that there's a seam in the talk

135
00:08:09.230 --> 00:08:12.410
[S02] and you can get back on.

136
00:08:12.410 --> 00:08:14.509
[S02] Okay?

137
00:08:14.509 --> 00:08:16.759
[S02] So now we're on a roll.

138
00:08:16.759 --> 00:08:19.329
[S02] And since we're on a roll,

139
00:08:19.329 --> 00:08:23.480
[S02] can you guess what fourth idea might be here?

140
00:08:23.480 --> 00:08:26.120
[S02] An idea that helps people get back on the bus?

141
00:08:32.889 --> 00:08:33.480
[S02] Yes?

142
00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:35.009
[S03] Asking a question.

143
00:08:35.009 --> 00:08:36.789
[S02] Ask a question, yes.

144
00:08:36.789 --> 00:08:39.309
[S02] Thank you.

145
00:08:39.309 --> 00:08:46.620
[S02] So ask a question.

146
00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:47.929
[S02] And so I will ask a question.

147
00:08:47.929 --> 00:08:50.230
[S02] How much dead air can there be?

148
00:08:50.230 --> 00:08:52.179
[S02] How long can I pause?

149
00:08:52.179 --> 00:08:54.230
[S02] I counted seven seconds.

150
00:08:54.230 --> 00:08:56.370
[S02] It seems like an eternity to me

151
00:08:56.370 --> 00:08:58.840
[S02] to wait and not say anything for seven seconds.

152
00:08:58.840 --> 00:09:00.929
[S02] But that's the standard amount of time

153
00:09:00.929 --> 00:09:02.059
[S02] you can wait for an answer.

154
00:09:02.059 --> 00:09:02.950
[S02] And of course, the question

155
00:09:02.950 --> 00:09:04.909
[S02] has to be carefully chosen.

156
00:09:04.909 --> 00:09:06.059
[S02] It can't be too obvious

157
00:09:06.059 --> 00:09:07.559
[S02] because then people will be embarrassed

158
00:09:07.559 --> 00:09:09.370
[S02] to say what the answer is.

159
00:09:09.370 --> 00:09:10.340
[S02] It can't be too hard

160
00:09:10.340 --> 00:09:13.789
[S02] because then nobody will have anything to say.

161
00:09:13.789 --> 00:09:15.929
[S02] So here are some sample heuristics

162
00:10:06.649 --> 00:10:10.080
[S04] So what do you think is a good time to have a lecture?

163
00:10:12.139 --> 00:10:13.379
[S04] 11:00 AM.

164
00:10:13.409 --> 00:10:14.409
[S04] Yeah.

165
00:10:19.419 --> 00:10:20.470
[S04] And the reason is,

166
00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:25.779
[S04] most people at MIT are awake by then,

167
00:10:25.809 --> 00:10:27.669
[S04] and hardly anyone has gone back to sleep.

168
00:10:29.379 --> 00:10:31.240
[S04] It's not right after a meal.

169
00:10:31.269 --> 00:10:33.740
[S04] People aren't fatigued from this or that.

170
00:10:33.769 --> 00:10:36.200
[S04] It's a great time to have a lecture.

171
00:10:36.230 --> 00:10:39.830
[S04] So that brings me next to the question of what about the place?

172
00:10:39.860 --> 00:10:42.549
[S04] And the most important thing about the place

173
00:10:42.580 --> 00:10:44.269
[S04] is that it be well lit.

174
00:10:50.470 --> 00:10:51.600
[S04] This room is well lit.

175
00:10:53.169 --> 00:10:57.279
[S04] Problem with the other kinds of rooms is that we humans,

176
00:10:57.309 --> 00:10:59.110
[S04] whenever the lights go down,

177
00:10:59.139 --> 00:11:02.110
[S04] or whenever the room is dimly lighted,

178
00:11:02.139 --> 00:11:04.549
[S04] it signals that we should go to sleep.

179
00:11:04.580 --> 00:11:07.639
[S04] So whenever I go somewhere to give a talk, even today,

180
00:11:07.669 --> 00:11:11.379
[S04] the first thing I do when I speak to the audiovisual people is say,

181
00:11:11.409 --> 00:11:12.570
[S04] "Keep the lights full up."

182
00:11:15.539 --> 00:11:16.799
[S04] Oh, they might reply,

183
00:11:18.669 --> 00:11:22.200
[S04] "People will see the slides better if we turn the lights off."

184
00:11:22.230 --> 00:11:23.799
[S04] And then I reply,

185
00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:26.570
[S04] "It's extremely hard to see slides through closed eyelids."

186
00:11:31.409 --> 00:11:34.240
[S04] What else can you say about the place?

187
00:11:34.269 --> 00:11:35.870
[S04] Well, the place should be cased.

188
00:11:39.179 --> 00:11:42.110
[S04] And I mean that in a colloquial sense of,

189
00:11:42.139 --> 00:11:44.379
[S04] like, if you're robbing a bank,

190
00:11:44.409 --> 00:11:46.379
[S04] you would go to the bank,

191
00:11:46.409 --> 00:11:49.570
[S04] you know, some occasions before to see what it's like,

192
00:11:49.600 --> 00:11:53.039
[S04] so there are no surprises when you do your robbery.

193
00:11:54.340 --> 00:11:56.940
[S04] So whenever I go somewhere to speak,

194
00:11:56.970 --> 00:11:58.799
[S04] the first thing I ask my host to do

195
00:11:58.830 --> 00:12:01.039
[S04] is to take me to the place where I'll be speaking,

196
00:12:01.070 --> 00:12:03.409
[S04] so that if there are any weirdnesses,

197
00:12:03.440 --> 00:12:07.539
[S04] I'll be able to deal with it.

198
00:12:07.570 --> 00:12:10.200
[S04] Sometimes it might require some intervention.

199
00:12:10.230 --> 00:12:11.600
[S04] Sometimes it just might require me

200
00:12:11.629 --> 00:12:14.830
[S04] to understand what the challenges are.

201
00:12:14.860 --> 00:12:18.110
[S04] So when I came here this morning,

202
00:12:18.139 --> 00:12:20.379
[S04] I did what I typically do.

203
00:12:20.409 --> 00:12:21.669
[S04] I imagined that all the seats

204
00:12:21.700 --> 00:12:23.870
[S04] were filled with disinterested farm animals.

205
00:12:25.509 --> 00:12:28.039
[S04] That way, I knew that no matter how bad it was,

206
00:12:28.070 --> 00:12:29.379
[S04] it wouldn't be as bad as that.

207
00:12:32.639 --> 00:12:36.669
[S04] So finally, it should be reasonably,

208
00:12:36.700 --> 00:12:38.799
[S04] it should be reasonably populated.

209
00:12:45.230 --> 00:12:47.200
[S04] It should be,

210
00:12:47.230 --> 00:12:48.269
[S04] it should be the case that,

211
00:12:48.299 --> 00:12:50.509
[S04] you know, if there are 10 people in this hall,

212
00:12:50.539 --> 00:12:52.669
[S04] everyone would be wondering what's going on

213
00:12:52.700 --> 00:12:55.379
[S04] that's so much more interesting that nobody's here.

214
00:12:55.409 --> 00:12:58.509
[S04] So you want to get a right-sized place that's,

215
00:12:58.539 --> 00:13:02.110
[S04] doesn't have to be packed, but it has to be more than half-full.

216
00:13:02.139 --> 00:13:05.669
[S04] So those are some thoughts about time and place.

217
00:13:05.700 --> 00:13:07.340
[S04] The next thing I want to talk about

218
00:13:07.370 --> 00:13:11.970
[S04] is the subject of boards and props and slides.

219
00:13:19.409 --> 00:13:22.200
[S04] Well, these are the tools of the trade.

220
00:13:22.230 --> 00:13:26.269
[S04] I believe that this is the,

221
00:13:26.299 --> 00:13:30.600
[S04] this is the right tool for speaking

222
00:13:30.629 --> 00:13:33.379
[S04] when your purpose is informing.

223
00:13:33.409 --> 00:13:37.470
[S04] The slides are good when your purpose is exposing.

224
00:13:37.500 --> 00:13:41.700
[S04] But this is what I use when I'm informing, teaching, lecturing.

225
00:13:41.740 --> 00:13:43.639
[S04] And there are several reasons why I use it.

226
00:13:43.669 --> 00:13:45.769
[S04] For one thing, when you use the board,

227
00:13:45.799 --> 00:13:47.139
[S04] you have a graphic quality.

228
00:13:51.539 --> 00:13:54.110
[S04] It's the case that when you have a board,

229
00:13:54.139 --> 00:13:58.799
[S04] then you can easily exploit the fact

230
00:13:58.830 --> 00:14:02.200
[S04] that you can use graphics in your presentation.

231
00:14:04.230 --> 00:14:05.940
[S04] So that's a graphic quality that I like.

232
00:14:05.970 --> 00:14:08.379
[S04] And the next thing I like is like a speed property.

233
00:14:11.509 --> 00:14:13.600
[S04] The speed with which you write on the blackboard

234
00:14:13.629 --> 00:14:14.940
[S04] is approximately the speed

235
00:14:14.970 --> 00:14:17.379
[S04] at which people can absorb ideas.

236
00:14:17.409 --> 00:14:20.200
[S04] If you go flipping through a bunch of slides,

237
00:14:20.230 --> 00:14:21.470
[S04] nobody can go that fast.

238
00:14:25.139 --> 00:14:28.379
[S04] Finally, one great property of a board

239
00:14:28.409 --> 00:14:30.039
[S04] is that it can be a target.

240
00:14:35.029 --> 00:14:40.379
[S04] Many people who are novices at speaking

241
00:14:40.409 --> 00:14:45.070
[S04] find themselves suddenly aware of their hands.

242
00:14:45.090 --> 00:14:47.200
[S04] It's as if their hands were private parts

243
00:14:47.230 --> 00:14:48.799
[S04] that shouldn't be exposed in public,

244
00:14:48.830 --> 00:14:52.009
[S04] so right away, they go into the pockets.

245
00:14:52.029 --> 00:14:53.379
[S04] And this is considered insulting

246
00:14:53.399 --> 00:14:55.440
[S04] in some parts of the world.

247
00:14:55.460 --> 00:15:00.799
[S04] Or alternatively, maybe the hands will go in back like this.

248
00:15:00.830 --> 00:15:04.799
[S04] I was once in a convent in Serbia.

249
00:15:05.169 --> 00:15:10.370
[S05] And my host, well, we were, as soon as we entered,

250
00:15:10.399 --> 00:15:13.460
[S05] a nun came up to us and offered us a refreshment.

251
00:15:13.490 --> 00:15:17.009
[S05] And I was about to say, "No, thank you," and he said,

252
00:15:17.039 --> 00:15:19.529
[S05] "Eat that stuff or die."

253
00:15:19.559 --> 00:15:23.570
[S05] It's a question of local custom and politeness.

254
00:15:23.600 --> 00:15:27.370
[S05] But then, before anything happened there,

255
00:15:27.399 --> 00:15:30.529
[S05] the nun pulled my hands off like this,

256
00:15:30.559 --> 00:15:33.490
[S05] because it was extraordinarily insulting in that culture

257
00:15:33.519 --> 00:15:36.049
[S05] to have your hands behind your back.

258
00:15:36.080 --> 00:15:37.250
[S05] So, why is that?

259
00:15:37.279 --> 00:15:40.370
[S05] Well, it's usually supposed that that's,

260
00:15:40.389 --> 00:15:43.320
[S05] that it has to do with whether you're concealing a weapon.

261
00:15:43.350 --> 00:15:47.009
[S05] So if your hands are in your pockets or behind your back,

262
00:15:47.039 --> 00:15:51.210
[S05] then it looks like you might have a weapon.

263
00:15:51.240 --> 00:15:53.169
[S05] And that's what I mean by the virtue,

264
00:15:53.200 --> 00:15:55.250
[S05] one of these virtues of the board.

265
00:15:55.279 --> 00:15:56.690
[S05] Now you have something to do with your hands.

266
00:15:56.720 --> 00:15:59.809
[S05] You can point out the stuff.

267
00:16:01.289 --> 00:16:03.490
[S05] I was once watching Seymour Papert give a lecture.

268
00:16:03.519 --> 00:16:05.250
[S05] I thought it was terrific.

269
00:16:05.279 --> 00:16:07.009
[S05] So I went a second time.

270
00:16:07.039 --> 00:16:08.809
[S05] First time to absorb the content,

271
00:16:08.840 --> 00:16:10.889
[S05] second time to note the style.

272
00:16:10.929 --> 00:16:12.490
[S05] And what I discovered is that Papert

273
00:16:12.519 --> 00:16:15.210
[S05] was constantly pointing at the board.

274
00:16:15.240 --> 00:16:16.570
[S05] And then I thought about it a little while,

275
00:16:16.600 --> 00:16:19.850
[S05] and I noted that none of the stuff he was pointing to

276
00:16:19.879 --> 00:16:23.330
[S05] had anything to do with what he was saying.

277
00:16:24.370 --> 00:16:28.289
[S05] Nevertheless, it was an effective technique.

278
00:16:30.370 --> 00:16:33.129
[S05] So that's just a little bit about the virtue

279
00:16:33.159 --> 00:16:34.409
[S05] of blackboards.

280
00:16:34.440 --> 00:16:37.330
[S05] Now, I want to talk about prompts.

281
00:16:37.360 --> 00:16:40.929
[S05] You know, the custodians of knowledge about prompts

282
00:16:40.960 --> 00:16:43.049
[S05] are the playwrights.

283
00:16:43.080 --> 00:16:46.889
[S05] Many decades ago, I saw a play by Heinrich Ibsen.

284
00:16:46.919 --> 00:16:48.730
[S05] It was Head of Gabler.

285
00:16:48.759 --> 00:16:50.529
[S05] I remember vaguely that it was about

286
00:16:50.559 --> 00:16:54.049
[S05] a woman in an unhappy marriage.

287
00:16:54.080 --> 00:16:56.490
[S05] And her husband was in competition

288
00:16:56.519 --> 00:16:58.889
[S05] for an academic job with somebody else.

289
00:16:58.919 --> 00:17:00.289
[S05] And he was going to lose,

290
00:17:00.320 --> 00:17:02.009
[S05] partly because he was boring

291
00:17:02.039 --> 00:17:03.970
[S05] and partly because the competitor

292
00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:06.930
[S05] had just written a magnificent book.

293
00:17:06.960 --> 00:17:08.369
[S05] By the way, this is back in the days

294
00:17:08.400 --> 00:17:11.410
[S05] before there were copying machines and computers.

295
00:17:11.440 --> 00:17:14.970
[S05] Anyhow, as the play opens,

296
00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:18.289
[S05] there's a pot-bellied stove.

297
00:17:24.759 --> 00:17:27.970
[S05] And in the beginning of the play,

298
00:17:28.000 --> 00:17:31.289
[S05] the pot-bellied stove with its open door

299
00:17:31.319 --> 00:17:35.480
[S05] just has some slightly glowing embers.

300
00:17:35.509 --> 00:17:38.130
[S05] But the pot-bellied stove is always there,

301
00:17:38.160 --> 00:17:40.609
[S05] and its tension mounts in the play.

302
00:17:40.640 --> 00:17:43.450
[S05] And you see this manuscript, this prop

303
00:17:43.480 --> 00:17:45.450
[S05] that Ibsen so artfully used.

304
00:17:45.480 --> 00:17:49.690
[S05] You just know that something's going to happen,

305
00:17:49.720 --> 00:17:52.089
[S05] because as the play goes on,

306
00:17:52.119 --> 00:17:55.009
[S05] the fire gets bigger and hotter,

307
00:17:55.039 --> 00:17:56.730
[S05] and finally, all-consuming,

308
00:17:56.759 --> 00:17:58.450
[S05] and you just know that that manuscript

309
00:17:58.480 --> 00:18:02.369
[S05] is going to go into that fire.

310
00:18:02.400 --> 00:18:03.250
[S05] It's memorable thing.

311
00:18:03.279 --> 00:18:07.210
[S05] It's what I remember about play.

312
00:18:07.240 --> 00:18:10.690
[S05] So, the playwrights have got this all figured out.

313
00:18:10.720 --> 00:18:12.089
[S05] But on the other hand,

314
00:18:12.119 --> 00:18:15.529
[S05] they're not the only people who can use prompts.

315
00:18:15.559 --> 00:18:18.730
[S05] Here's an example of the use of a prop,

316
00:18:18.759 --> 00:18:20.890
[S05] also due to Seymour Papert.

317
00:18:20.920 --> 00:18:22.930
[S05] He was talking about how it's important

318
00:18:22.960 --> 00:18:25.970
[S05] to look at the problem in the right way.

319
00:18:26.000 --> 00:18:29.490
[S05] And here's an example that not only teaches that,

320
00:18:29.519 --> 00:18:31.529
[S05] but makes it possible for you

321
00:18:31.559 --> 00:18:34.369
[S05] to embarrass your friends in mechanical engineering.

322
00:18:34.400 --> 00:18:36.210
[S05] So here's what you do.

323
00:18:36.240 --> 00:18:37.930
[S05] Take this bicycle wheel.

324
00:18:37.960 --> 00:18:39.329
[S05] You start it spinning.

325
00:18:39.359 --> 00:18:43.130
[S05] And then you put some torque on the axle.

326
00:18:43.160 --> 00:18:47.369
[S05] Or equivalently, you blow on the edge.

327
00:18:47.400 --> 00:18:50.730
[S05] And the issue is, does it go that way?

328
00:18:50.759 --> 00:18:53.930
[S05] Or does it go that way?

329
00:18:53.960 --> 00:18:56.250
[S05] Now, the mechanical engineers

330
00:18:56.279 --> 00:18:58.089
[S05] will immediately say, "Oh, yes, I see.

331
00:18:58.119 --> 00:18:59.410
[S05] Right-hand screw wheel."

332
00:18:59.440 --> 00:19:02.089
[S05] And they'll put their fingers in this position,

333
00:19:02.119 --> 00:19:05.250
[S05] but forget exactly how to align their fingers

334
00:19:05.279 --> 00:19:08.970
[S05] with the various aspects of the problem.

335
00:19:09.000 --> 00:19:10.609
[S05] And so it's usually the case

336
00:19:10.640 --> 00:19:15.410
[S05] that they get it right with about a 50% probability.

337
00:19:15.440 --> 00:19:19.329
[S05] So their very fancy education

338
00:19:19.359 --> 00:19:20.289
[S05] gets them up to the point

339
00:19:20.319 --> 00:19:24.240
[S05] where they're equivalent to flipping a coin.

340
00:19:24.269 --> 00:19:26.849
[S05] But it doesn't have to be that way,

341
00:19:26.869 --> 00:19:28.490
[S05] because you can think about the problem

342
00:20:09.200 --> 00:20:11.630
[S06] And so now you'll never wonder again.

343
00:20:11.630 --> 00:20:14.170
[S06] Because you're thinking about the problem in the right way.

344
00:20:14.170 --> 00:20:20.130
[S06] And it's demonstrated by the use of a prop.

345
00:20:20.130 --> 00:20:25.839
[S06] You can try this after we're done.

346
00:20:25.839 --> 00:20:32.039
[S06] Another example I like to remember is one from when I was taking 801.

347
00:20:32.039 --> 00:20:35.180
[S06] Alan Lazarus was the instructor at the time.

348
00:20:35.180 --> 00:20:37.980
[S06] And he was talking about the conservation of energy.

349
00:20:37.980 --> 00:20:40.779
[S06] Kinetic and potential.

350
00:20:40.779 --> 00:20:45.180
[S06] And there was a long wire in the ceiling in 26100

351
00:20:45.180 --> 00:20:48.109
[S06] attached to a much bigger steel ball.

352
00:20:48.109 --> 00:20:50.710
[S06] The one not like this.

353
00:20:50.710 --> 00:20:56.329
[S06] And Lazarus took the ball up against the wall like this.

354
00:20:56.329 --> 00:21:00.390
[S06] He put his head flat against the wall to steady himself.

355
00:21:00.390 --> 00:21:02.920
[S06] And then he let go.

356
00:21:02.920 --> 00:21:06.920
[S06] And the pendulum takes many seconds to go over and back.

357
00:21:06.920 --> 00:21:12.490
[S06] And then gently kisses Lazarus's nose.

358
00:21:12.490 --> 00:21:24.380
[S06] And so you have many seconds to think, this guy really believes in the conservation of energy.

359
00:21:24.380 --> 00:21:27.819
[S06] Do not try this at home.

360
00:21:27.819 --> 00:21:33.029
[S06] The problem is that, first time you do this, you may not just let go.

361
00:21:33.029 --> 00:21:42.740
[S06] There's a natural human tendency to push.

362
00:21:42.740 --> 00:22:05.359
[S06] So that's a little bit on a subject of props.

363
00:22:05.359 --> 00:22:06.539
[S06] You know, it's interesting.

364
00:22:06.539 --> 00:22:12.970
[S06] Whenever surveys are taken, students always say, more chalk, less PowerPoint.

365
00:22:12.970 --> 00:22:15.339
[S06] And why would that be?

366
00:22:15.339 --> 00:22:18.230
[S06] Props are always also very effective.

367
00:22:18.230 --> 00:22:20.349
[S06] Why would that be?

368
00:22:20.349 --> 00:22:24.039
[S06] I'll give you my lunatic fringe view on this.

369
00:22:24.039 --> 00:22:41.440
[S06] It has to do with what I would call

370
00:22:41.440 --> 00:22:43.579
[S06] empathetic mirroring.

371
00:22:43.579 --> 00:22:46.190
[S06] When you're sitting up there watching me write on the board,

372
00:22:46.190 --> 00:22:51.539
[S06] all those little mirror neurons in your head, I believe, become actuated.

373
00:22:51.539 --> 00:22:54.269
[S06] And you can feel yourself writing on the blackboard.

374
00:22:54.269 --> 00:23:00.579
[S06] And even more so, when I talk about this steel ball going that way and this way,

375
00:23:00.579 --> 00:23:05.130
[S06] you can feel the ball as if you were me.

376
00:23:05.130 --> 00:23:07.579
[S06] And you can't do that with a slide.

377
00:23:07.579 --> 00:23:09.269
[S06] You can't do it with a picture.

378
00:23:09.269 --> 00:23:12.920
[S06] You need to see it in a physical world.

379
00:23:12.920 --> 00:23:15.269
[S06] That's why I think that...

380
00:23:15.269 --> 00:23:20.880
[S06] Oh, yes, of course, there are speed questions involved too that have to be separated out.

381
00:23:20.880 --> 00:23:29.079
[S06] But I think the empathetic mirroring is why props and the use of a blackboard are so effective.

382
00:23:29.079 --> 00:23:32.920
[S06] Well, let's see.

383
00:23:32.920 --> 00:23:37.849
[S06] Oh, yes, there is one more thing by way of the tools.

384
00:23:37.849 --> 00:23:43.440
[S06] And that has to do with the use of slides.

385
00:23:43.440 --> 00:23:47.470
[S06] I repeat, I think they're for exposing ideas, not for teaching ideas.

386
00:23:47.470 --> 00:23:52.380
[S06] But that's what we do in a job talk or a conference talk.

387
00:23:52.380 --> 00:23:55.190
[S06] We expose ideas, we don't teach them.

388
00:23:55.190 --> 00:24:01.579
[S06] So let me tell you a little bit about my views on that.

389
00:24:01.579 --> 00:24:10.230
[S06] I remember once I was in Terminal A at Logan Airport.

390
00:24:10.230 --> 00:24:14.420
[S06] I'd just come back from a really miserable conference.

391
00:24:14.420 --> 00:24:16.690
[S06] And the flight was really horrible.

392
00:24:16.690 --> 00:24:20.230
[S06] It was one of those that feels like an unbalanced washing machine.

393
00:24:20.230 --> 00:24:25.690
[S06] And for the only time in my life, I decided to stop on my way to my car

394
00:24:25.690 --> 00:24:29.819
[S06] and have a cup of coffee and relax a little bit.

395
00:24:29.819 --> 00:24:35.880
[S06] And as I was there for a few minutes, someone came up to me and said, are you Professor Winston?

396
00:24:35.880 --> 00:24:39.130
[S06] "I think so," I said.

397
00:24:39.130 --> 00:24:41.769
[S06] I don't know, I guess I was trying to be funny.

398
00:24:41.769 --> 00:24:45.539
[S06] In any event, he said, "I'm on my way to Europe to give a job talk.

399
00:24:45.539 --> 00:24:48.690
[S06] Would you mind critiquing my slides?"

400
00:24:48.690 --> 00:24:49.809
[S06] "Not at all," I said.

401
00:24:49.809 --> 00:24:53.960
[S06] "You have too many and they have too many words."

402
00:24:53.960 --> 00:24:58.960
[S06] "How did you know?" he said, thinking perhaps I'd seen a talk of his before.

403
00:24:58.960 --> 00:25:01.000
[S06] I hadn't.

404
00:25:01.000 --> 00:25:04.000
[S06] My reply was, because it's always true.

405
00:25:04.000 --> 00:25:07.539
[S06] There are always too many slides, always too many words.

406
00:25:07.539 --> 00:25:12.150
[S06] So let me show you some extreme examples of how not to use slides.

407
00:25:15.160 --> 00:25:23.299
[S07] Well, for this demonstration, I need to be way over here.

408
00:25:23.299 --> 00:25:29.599
[S07] And when I get over here, then I can start to say things like,

409
00:25:29.599 --> 00:25:32.579
[S07] "One of the things you shouldn't do is read your transparencies.

410
00:25:32.579 --> 00:25:36.470
[S07] People in your audience know how to read, and reading will just annoy them."

411
00:25:36.470 --> 00:25:40.710
[S07] "Also, you should be sure that you have only a few words on each transparency,

412
00:25:40.710 --> 00:25:43.259
[S07] and that the words are easy to read."

413
00:25:43.259 --> 00:25:45.839
[S07] And I hope it's driving you crazy.

414
00:25:45.839 --> 00:25:48.980
[S07] Because I'm committing all kinds of crimes.

415
00:25:48.980 --> 00:25:53.650
[S07] The first of which is that there are too many words on this slide.

416
00:25:53.650 --> 00:25:57.700
[S07] Second of which is I'm way over there, and the slide's way over there.

417
00:25:57.700 --> 00:26:04.829
[S07] So you get into this tennis match feeling of shifting back and forth between the slide

418
00:26:04.829 --> 00:26:05.829
[S07] and the speaker.

419
00:26:05.829 --> 00:26:10.329
[S07] You want the slides to be condiments to what you're saying, not the main event or the

420
00:26:10.329 --> 00:26:12.200
[S07] opposite way around.

421
00:26:12.200 --> 00:26:14.700
[S07] So how can we fix this?

422
00:26:14.700 --> 00:26:19.029
[S07] Step number one is to get rid of the background junk.

423
00:26:19.029 --> 00:26:21.869
[S07] It's always distraction.

424
00:26:21.869 --> 00:26:24.869
[S07] Step number two is to get rid of the words.

425
00:26:24.869 --> 00:26:32.049
[S07] When I reduce the words to these, then everything I read a previous time, I'm not licensed

426
00:26:32.049 --> 00:26:34.400
[S07] to say because it's not on the slide.

427
00:26:34.400 --> 00:26:39.730
[S07] I'm not reading my slides anymore, but I'm saying what was written on the slides in a

428
00:26:39.730 --> 00:26:43.160
[S07] previous example.

429
00:26:43.160 --> 00:26:45.910
[S07] So what else can we do to simplify this?

430
00:26:45.910 --> 00:26:48.940
[S07] Well, we can get rid of the logos.

431
00:26:48.940 --> 00:26:52.230
[S07] We don't need them.

432
00:26:52.230 --> 00:26:54.259
[S07] Simplification.

433
00:26:54.259 --> 00:26:56.660
[S07] What else can we do?

434
00:26:56.660 --> 00:26:58.660
[S07] Get rid of the title.

435
00:26:58.660 --> 00:27:02.369
[S07] "Now I want to talk to you about some rules for slide preparation."

436
00:27:02.369 --> 00:27:04.009
[S07] I'm telling you the title.

437
00:27:04.009 --> 00:27:06.049
[S07] It doesn't have to be up there.

438
00:27:06.049 --> 00:27:10.299
[S07] By reducing the number of words on the slide, I'm allowing you to pay more attention to

439
00:27:10.299 --> 00:27:13.549
[S07] me and less to what's written on the slide.

440
00:27:13.549 --> 00:27:18.230
[S07] I mentioned it before, we have only one language processor, and we can either use it to read

441
00:27:18.230 --> 00:27:21.299
[S07] stuff or to listen to the speaker.

442
00:27:21.299 --> 00:27:26.410
[S07] And so if we have too many words on the slide, it forces people in the audience to read

443
00:27:26.410 --> 00:27:30.599
[S07] this stuff and not listen.

444
00:27:30.599 --> 00:27:33.880
[S07] A student of mine did an experiment a few years ago.

445
00:27:33.880 --> 00:27:41.380
[S07] He taught some students some web-based programming ideas.

446
00:27:41.380 --> 00:27:43.450
[S07] Half the information was on the slides.

447
00:27:43.450 --> 00:27:45.630
[S07] He said the other half.

448
00:27:45.630 --> 00:27:48.809
[S07] And then for a control group, he reversed it.

449
00:27:48.809 --> 00:27:54.410
[S07] And the question was, what did the subjects, that is, say, freshman in his fraternity,

450
00:27:54.410 --> 00:27:57.339
[S07] what did the subjects remember best?

451
00:27:57.339 --> 00:28:02.450
[S07] What he said or what they read on the slide?

452
00:28:02.450 --> 00:28:04.559
[S07] And the answer is?

453
00:28:04.559 --> 00:28:08.059
[S07] What they read on the slide.

454
00:28:08.059 --> 00:28:11.200
[S07] When their slides have a lot of material on it, they don't pay attention to the speaker.

455
00:28:11.200 --> 00:28:16.809
[S07] In fact, in the after-action report, one of the subjects said, "I wish you hadn't talked

456
00:28:16.809 --> 00:28:17.809
[S07] so much.

457
00:28:17.809 --> 00:28:22.119
[S07] It was distracting."

458
00:28:22.119 --> 00:28:25.140
[S07] Well, the last item is eliminate clutter.

459
00:28:25.140 --> 00:28:26.619
[S07] I hear some clutter.

460
00:28:26.619 --> 00:28:30.980
[S07] No reason even for those bullets.

461
00:28:30.980 --> 00:28:37.029
[S07] So the too-many-words problem is a consequence of a crime Microsoft has committed by allowing

462
00:28:37.029 --> 00:28:40.500
[S07] you to use fonts that are too small.

463
00:28:40.500 --> 00:28:46.809
[S07] So you should all have a sample slide like this that you can use to determine what the

464
00:28:46.809 --> 00:28:50.619
[S07] minimum font size is that's easily measurable.

465
00:28:50.619 --> 00:28:54.700
[S07] Shiro, what do you think of those?

466
00:28:54.700 --> 00:28:57.670
[S08] Which size is right?

467
00:28:57.670 --> 00:28:58.670
[S07] What's that?

468
00:28:58.670 --> 00:28:59.670
[S08] Which size is right?

469
00:28:59.670 --> 00:29:00.670
[S07] Yeah.

470
00:29:00.670 --> 00:29:01.670
[S07] Minimum, maybe.

471
00:29:01.670 --> 00:29:03.980
[S08] Forty or fifty?

472
00:29:03.980 --> 00:29:06.119
[S07] Yeah, he says forty or fifty.

473
00:29:06.119 --> 00:29:08.450
[S07] I think that's about right.

474
00:29:08.450 --> 00:29:10.200
[S07] Thirty-five is if you get too small.

475
00:29:10.200 --> 00:29:14.839
[S07] Not necessarily because you can't read it, but because you're probably using it to get

476
00:29:14.839 --> 00:29:18.619
[S07] too many words on the slide.

477
00:29:18.619 --> 00:29:21.000
[S07] What other crimes do we have?

478
00:29:21.000 --> 00:29:23.809
[S07] Well, we have the laser pointer crime.

479
00:29:23.809 --> 00:29:29.230
[S07] And for that, I believe, you know, in the old days, when we didn't have laser pointers,

480
00:29:29.230 --> 00:29:37.130
[S07] we used wooden ones, and people would go waving these things around, and...

481
00:29:37.130 --> 00:29:40.769
[S07] Pretty soon, it became almost like a tan swirling contest.

482
00:29:40.769 --> 00:29:50.309
[S07] So here's what I recommended in the old days for dealing with this kind of pointer.

483
00:29:50.309 --> 00:29:55.369
[S07] This is an example of the use of a prop.

484
00:29:55.369 --> 00:30:00.099
[S07] Jim Glass up there saw this talk about 20 years ago, and said, "Oh, yeah, I remember

485
00:30:00.099 --> 00:30:01.099
[S07] that talk.

486
00:30:01.099 --> 00:30:04.460
[S07] That's the one where you broke the pointer."

487
00:30:04.460 --> 00:30:08.630
[S07] It's amazing how props tend to be the things that are remembered.

488
00:30:08.630 --> 00:30:13.309
[S07] But now, we don't have penal pointers anymore.

489
00:30:13.309 --> 00:30:14.309
[S07] We've got...

490
00:30:15.109 --> 00:30:16.970
[S09] We've got laser pointers.

491
00:30:17.000 --> 00:30:22.410
[S09] It's a wonder more people aren't driven into epileptic fits over this sort of stuff.

492
00:30:22.440 --> 00:30:25.210
[S09] But here's what tends to happen.

493
00:30:25.240 --> 00:30:31.400
[S09] Look at that, it's a lovely recursive picture and I can become part of it by putting

494
00:30:31.430 --> 00:30:37.710
[S09] that laser beam right on the back of my head up there.

495
00:30:37.740 --> 00:30:39.769
[S09] And what do you see?

496
00:30:39.799 --> 00:30:42.170
[S09] You see the back of my head?

497
00:30:42.200 --> 00:30:45.920
[S09] I have no eye contact, no engagement, nothing.

498
00:30:45.950 --> 00:30:51.480
[S09] I was sitting with a student watching a talk one day and she said, "You know what?

499
00:30:51.509 --> 00:30:57.309
[S09] We could all leave and he wouldn't know."

500
00:30:57.339 --> 00:30:59.150
[S09] So what happens when you use a laser pointer?

501
00:30:59.180 --> 00:31:04.369
[S09] You can't use a laser pointer without turning your head and pointing it at something.

502
00:31:04.400 --> 00:31:08.750
[S09] And when you do that, you lose contact with the audience.

503
00:31:08.779 --> 00:31:10.680
[S09] You don't want to do it.

504
00:31:10.710 --> 00:31:15.250
[S09] So what do you do if you need to identify something in your image and you don't want

505
00:31:15.279 --> 00:31:16.470
[S09] to point at it with a laser?

506
00:31:16.500 --> 00:31:18.450
[S09] This is what you do.

507
00:31:18.480 --> 00:31:21.390
[S09] Put a little arrow on there and say, "Now, look at that guy number one," at the end

508
00:31:21.420 --> 00:31:23.339
[S09] of arrow number one.

509
00:31:23.369 --> 00:31:30.500
[S09] You don't need to have a laser pointer to do that.

510
00:31:30.529 --> 00:31:32.890
[S09] The too-heavy crime.

511
00:31:32.920 --> 00:31:38.470
[S09] When people ask me to review a presentation, I ask them to print it out and lay it out

512
00:31:38.500 --> 00:31:42.210
[S09] on a table.

513
00:31:42.240 --> 00:31:46.819
[S09] When they do that, it's easy to see whether the talk is too heavy.

514
00:31:46.849 --> 00:31:54.009
[S09] Too much text, not enough air, not enough white space, not enough imagery.

515
00:31:54.039 --> 00:31:57.750
[S09] This is a good example of such a talk.

516
00:31:57.779 --> 00:31:59.890
[S09] Way too heavy.

517
00:31:59.920 --> 00:32:05.539
[S09] The presenter has taken advantage of small font sizes to get as much on the slide as

518
00:32:05.569 --> 00:32:08.779
[S09] he wanted.

519
00:32:08.809 --> 00:32:13.470
[S09] Lots of other crimes here, but the too heaviness, the fact that it's too heavy is what I wanted

520
00:32:13.500 --> 00:32:15.079
[S09] to illustrate.

521
00:32:15.109 --> 00:32:19.650
[S09] So here, by contrast, is another talk, one I gave a few years ago.

522
00:32:19.680 --> 00:32:27.960
[S09] It wasn't a deeply technical talk, but I show it to you because there's air in it.

523
00:32:27.990 --> 00:32:30.299
[S09] It's mostly pictures of things.

524
00:32:30.329 --> 00:32:35.170
[S09] There are three or four slides that have text on them, but when I come to those, I

525
00:32:35.200 --> 00:32:37.740
[S09] give the audience time to read them.

526
00:32:37.769 --> 00:32:41.920
[S09] And they're there because they might have some historical significance.

527
00:32:41.950 --> 00:32:48.740
[S09] The first slide with a lot of text on it is an extraction from the 1957, from the proposal

528
00:32:48.769 --> 00:32:52.049
[S09] for the 1957 AI conference at Dartmouth.

529
00:32:52.079 --> 00:32:59.190
[S09] Extraordinarily interesting event and that historical extraction from the proposal helps

530
00:32:59.220 --> 00:33:02.140
[S09] drive that point home.

531
00:33:02.170 --> 00:33:04.119
[S09] What else have we got here?

532
00:33:04.150 --> 00:33:06.859
[S09] Oh yeah, your vocabulary word for the day.

533
00:33:06.890 --> 00:33:09.930
[S09] This is an epax legomanon.

534
00:33:09.960 --> 00:33:14.640
[S09] What that means is, this is the kind of slide you can get away with exactly once in your

535
00:33:14.670 --> 00:33:17.700
[S09] presentation.

536
00:33:17.730 --> 00:33:23.339
[S09] This is a slide that got some currency some years ago because it shows the complexity

537
00:33:23.369 --> 00:33:30.779
[S09] of governing in Afghanistan by showing how impossibly complex it is.

538
00:33:30.809 --> 00:33:35.809
[S09] It's something you and the audience can't understand and that's the point.

539
00:33:35.839 --> 00:33:37.140
[S09] But you can't have many of these.

540
00:33:37.170 --> 00:33:41.930
[S09] You can have one per work, one per presentation, one per paper, one per book.

541
00:33:41.960 --> 00:33:48.049
[S09] That's what epax legomanon is and this is an example of it.

542
00:33:48.079 --> 00:33:52.250
[S09] Well, I've shown you some crimes and so you might be asking, "Do these crimes actually

543
00:33:52.279 --> 00:33:53.839
[S09] occur?"

544
00:33:53.869 --> 00:34:02.960
[S09] So, they do.

545
00:34:02.990 --> 00:34:07.740
[S09] There's the hands in the pockets crime.

546
00:34:07.769 --> 00:34:13.409
[S09] There's a crime in time and place selection here.

547
00:34:13.440 --> 00:34:17.050
[S09] This is how you get to the Bartos Theater.

548
00:34:17.079 --> 00:34:21.500
[S09] First thing you do is you get on these steps over at the media lab.

549
00:34:21.530 --> 00:34:25.340
[S09] Then you cross this large, open space.

550
00:34:25.369 --> 00:34:28.809
[S09] Then you turn right down this corridor.

551
00:34:28.840 --> 00:34:32.489
[S09] At this point, whenever I go in there, I wonder if there are torture implements around

552
00:34:32.519 --> 00:34:33.949
[S09] the corner.

553
00:34:33.969 --> 00:34:38.849
[S09] And then when you get in there, you get into this dark, gloomy place.

554
00:34:38.869 --> 00:34:42.780
[S09] So it's well-named when they call it the Bartos Theater because it's a place where

555
00:34:42.809 --> 00:34:48.530
[S09] you can watch a movie, but it's not a place where you can give a talk.

556
00:34:48.550 --> 00:34:51.780
[S09] Now, on a subject of does it happen?

557
00:34:51.809 --> 00:34:56.139
[S09] Here's a talk I attended a while back in Stata.

558
00:34:56.159 --> 00:35:00.349
[S09] Notice that the speaker is far away from the slides.

559
00:35:00.369 --> 00:35:03.929
[S09] Speaker's using a laser pointer.

560
00:35:03.960 --> 00:35:07.349
[S09] And you say to me, "Well, what's happening here?"

561
00:35:07.369 --> 00:35:08.659
[S09] It's, by the way, the

562
00:35:09.119 --> 00:35:12.469
[S10] Eightieth slide in the presentation.

563
00:35:12.500 --> 00:35:14.409
[S10] Notice that it's dense with words.

564
00:35:14.440 --> 00:35:16.739
[S10] This is the first of ten conclusion slides.

565
00:35:19.150 --> 00:35:21.139
[S10] So what's the audience's reaction?

566
00:35:22.940 --> 00:35:24.469
[S10] That's the sponsor of the meeting.

567
00:35:25.940 --> 00:35:26.869
[S10] He's reading his email.

568
00:35:28.550 --> 00:35:31.340
[S10] This is the co-sponsor of the meeting.

569
00:35:31.369 --> 00:35:32.869
[S10] He's examining the lunch menu.

570
00:35:35.780 --> 00:35:36.769
[S10] What about this person?

571
00:35:40.019 --> 00:35:42.369
[S10] This person looks like he's paying attention.

572
00:35:43.469 --> 00:35:45.369
[S10] But just because it's a still picture.

573
00:35:46.239 --> 00:35:49.469
[S10] If you were to see a video, what you would see is something like this.

574
00:35:55.579 --> 00:35:57.769
[S10] So yeah, it does happen.

575
00:35:59.619 --> 00:36:04.239
[S10] Well, now, that's a quick review of tools.

576
00:36:04.269 --> 00:36:05.469
[S10] Now I want to talk about

577
00:36:07.769 --> 00:36:08.969
[S10] some special cases.

578
00:36:11.820 --> 00:36:13.969
[S10] We could talk a little bit about informing,

579
00:36:14.000 --> 00:36:17.570
[S10] or to say it another way, doing what I'm doing now.

580
00:36:19.269 --> 00:36:21.340
[S10] But I'll just say a few words about that.

581
00:36:21.369 --> 00:36:23.469
[S10] In that kind of presentation,

582
00:36:23.500 --> 00:36:24.710
[S10] you want to start with a promise,

583
00:36:24.739 --> 00:36:29.070
[S10] like I did for this hour that we're going now.

584
00:36:29.769 --> 00:36:32.170
[S10] But then it comes to the question of how do you inspire people?

585
00:36:34.239 --> 00:36:35.670
[S10] I've given this talk for a long time.

586
00:36:35.699 --> 00:36:37.269
[S10] And a few years ago,

587
00:36:38.699 --> 00:36:39.710
[S10] our department chairman said,

588
00:36:39.739 --> 00:36:43.409
[S10] "Would you please give this talk to a new faculty

589
00:36:43.440 --> 00:36:47.170
[S10] and be sure to emphasize what it takes to inspire students?"

590
00:36:47.969 --> 00:36:50.409
[S10] And strangely, I hadn't thought about that question before,

591
00:36:50.440 --> 00:36:52.440
[S10] so I started a survey.

592
00:36:52.469 --> 00:36:55.670
[S10] I talked to some of my incoming freshman advisees,

593
00:36:55.699 --> 00:36:59.670
[S10] and I talked to senior faculty and everything in between

594
00:36:59.699 --> 00:37:01.170
[S10] about how they've been inspired.

595
00:37:02.710 --> 00:37:05.210
[S10] What I found from the incoming freshmen

596
00:37:05.239 --> 00:37:07.469
[S10] is that they were inspired by some high school teacher

597
00:37:07.500 --> 00:37:08.570
[S10] who told them they could do it.

598
00:37:10.210 --> 00:37:11.869
[S10] What I found in the senior faculty,

599
00:37:13.340 --> 00:37:15.769
[S10] they were inspired by someone

600
00:37:15.800 --> 00:37:18.269
[S10] who helped them to see a problem in a new way.

601
00:37:19.469 --> 00:37:21.969
[S10] And then what I saw from everyone

602
00:37:22.000 --> 00:37:23.670
[S10] is that they were inspired

603
00:37:25.070 --> 00:37:29.269
[S10] when someone exhibited passion about what they were doing.

604
00:37:30.869 --> 00:37:32.469
[S10] Exhibited passion about what they were doing.

605
00:37:33.869 --> 00:37:37.869
[S10] Yeah, so that's one way to be inspiring.

606
00:37:38.869 --> 00:37:41.570
[S10] It's easy for me because, you know,

607
00:37:41.599 --> 00:37:43.070
[S10] I do artificial intelligence.

608
00:37:44.570 --> 00:37:48.469
[S10] And how can you not be interested in artificial intelligence?

609
00:37:49.210 --> 00:37:52.070
[S10] I mean, if you're not interested in artificial intelligence,

610
00:37:52.099 --> 00:37:54.269
[S10] you're probably not interested in interesting things.

611
00:37:56.820 --> 00:38:00.909
[S10] So when I'm lecturing in my AI class,

612
00:38:00.940 --> 00:38:03.769
[S10] it's natural for me to talk about what I think is cool

613
00:38:04.739 --> 00:38:07.670
[S10] and how exciting some new idea is.

614
00:38:08.570 --> 00:38:12.269
[S10] So that's the kind of expression of passion

615
00:38:12.300 --> 00:38:15.670
[S10] that makes a difference while informing

616
00:38:15.699 --> 00:38:18.369
[S10] with respect to this question of inspiring.

617
00:38:21.139 --> 00:38:24.670
[S10] Oh, yeah, and of course, during this promise phase,

618
00:38:24.699 --> 00:38:28.269
[S10] you can also express how cool stuff is.

619
00:38:28.300 --> 00:38:31.769
[S10] Let me give you an example of a lecture that starts this way.

620
00:38:31.800 --> 00:38:34.170
[S10] I'm talking about resource allocation.

621
00:38:34.199 --> 00:38:36.469
[S10] It's the same sort of stuff you would think of when you're

622
00:38:36.500 --> 00:38:38.409
[S10] it's the same sort of ideas you would need

623
00:38:38.440 --> 00:38:42.469
[S10] if you're allocating aircraft to a flight schedule

624
00:38:42.500 --> 00:38:45.070
[S10] or trying to schedule a factory or something like that.

625
00:38:45.099 --> 00:38:48.570
[S10] But the example is of putting colors on the states,

626
00:38:48.599 --> 00:38:49.969
[S10] the United States,

627
00:38:50.000 --> 00:38:52.469
[S10] without any bordering states having the same color.

628
00:38:52.500 --> 00:38:53.269
[S10] So here goes.

629
00:38:56.619 --> 00:38:59.769
[S10] This is what I show in the beginning of the class.

630
00:38:59.800 --> 00:39:01.769
[S10] This is a way of doing that coloring.

631
00:39:02.739 --> 00:39:04.969
[S10] And you might say, "Well, why don't we wait till it finishes?"

632
00:39:06.639 --> 00:39:07.469
[S10] Would you like to do that?

633
00:39:08.469 --> 00:39:09.269
[S10] No?

634
00:39:10.969 --> 00:39:12.409
[S10] Well, we're not going to wait till it finishes

635
00:39:12.440 --> 00:39:14.170
[S10] because the sun will have exploded

636
00:39:14.199 --> 00:39:16.670
[S10] and consumed the Earth before this program finishes.

637
00:39:18.269 --> 00:39:22.269
[S10] But with a slight adjustment to how the program works,

638
00:39:22.300 --> 00:39:23.469
[S10] which I tell my students,

639
00:39:23.500 --> 00:39:26.070
[S10] "You will understand in the next 50 minutes,

640
00:39:26.099 --> 00:39:26.869
[S10] this is what you get."

641
00:39:30.969 --> 00:39:32.269
[S10] Isn't that cool?

642
00:39:32.300 --> 00:39:35.769
[S10] You know, you've got to be amazed by stuff

643
00:39:35.800 --> 00:39:37.409
[S10] that takes a computation

644
00:39:37.429 --> 00:39:39.969
[S10] from longer than the lifetime of the solar system

645
00:39:40.000 --> 00:39:40.969
[S10] into a few seconds.

646
00:39:42.119 --> 00:39:43.070
[S10] So that's what I mean by

647
00:39:45.269 --> 00:39:46.969
[S10] providing a promise upfront

648
00:39:47.000 --> 00:39:49.369
[S10] and expressing some passion about what you're talking about.

649
00:39:51.619 --> 00:39:53.570
[S10] Well, the last item in this little block here

650
00:39:55.119 --> 00:39:57.869
[S10] has to do with what people think that they do at MIT.

651
00:40:07.269 --> 00:40:10.280
[S11] And then you say, "Oh, that's great.

652
00:40:10.280 --> 00:40:13.239
[S11] How do you teach people how to think?"

653
00:40:13.239 --> 00:40:16.480
[S11] Blank stare.

654
00:40:16.480 --> 00:40:21.949
[S11] No one can quite respond to that part, that natural next question.

655
00:40:21.949 --> 00:40:23.510
[S11] So how do you teach people how to think?

656
00:40:23.510 --> 00:40:30.030
[S11] Well, I believe that we are storytelling animals.

657
00:40:30.030 --> 00:40:36.280
[S11] And that we start developing our story, understanding and manipulating skills with fairy tales

658
00:40:36.280 --> 00:40:43.730
[S11] and childhood, and continue on through professional schools like law, business, medicine, engineering,

659
00:40:43.730 --> 00:40:45.090
[S11] everything.

660
00:40:45.090 --> 00:40:47.940
[S11] And we continue doing that throughout life.

661
00:40:47.940 --> 00:40:52.829
[S11] So if that is what thinking is all about, then when you want to teach people how to

662
00:40:52.829 --> 00:40:56.489
[S11] think, you provide them with the stories they need to know, the questions they need

663
00:40:56.489 --> 00:41:01.579
[S11] to ask about those stories, mechanisms for analyzing those stories, ways of putting stories

664
00:41:01.579 --> 00:41:07.510
[S11] together, ways of evaluating how reliable a story is, and that's what I think you need

665
00:41:07.510 --> 00:41:11.429
[S11] to do when you teach people how to think.

666
00:41:11.429 --> 00:41:18.650
[S11] But that's all about education, and many of you here, not necessarily for that, but

667
00:41:18.650 --> 00:41:24.820
[S11] rather for this part, for persuading, which breaks down into several categories: oral

668
00:41:24.820 --> 00:41:29.429
[S11] exams, not shown, job talks, getting famous.

669
00:41:29.429 --> 00:41:34.369
[S11] I won't say much about oral exams, other than the fact that they used to be a lot scarier

670
00:41:34.369 --> 00:41:36.150
[S11] than they are today.

671
00:41:36.150 --> 00:41:42.760
[S11] In the old days, reading the literature in a foreign language was a part of that, and

672
00:41:42.760 --> 00:41:45.570
[S11] there was a high failure rate.

673
00:41:45.570 --> 00:41:52.960
[S11] And when you look back on those failures, the most usual reason for people failing

674
00:41:52.960 --> 00:41:58.760
[S11] an oral exam is failure to situate and a failure to practice.

675
00:41:58.760 --> 00:42:03.829
[S11] By situate, I mean it's important to talk about your research in context.

676
00:42:03.829 --> 00:42:06.760
[S11] This is a problem that's being pursued all over the world.

677
00:42:06.760 --> 00:42:11.760
[S11] There hasn't been any progress before me in the past 30 years.

678
00:42:11.760 --> 00:42:16.590
[S11] Everyone is looking for a solution because it will have impact on so many other things.

679
00:42:16.590 --> 00:42:20.179
[S11] So that's situating in time and place and field.

680
00:42:20.179 --> 00:42:25.340
[S11] And then as far as practice is concerned, yes, practice is important, but that doesn't

681
00:42:25.340 --> 00:42:31.590
[S11] mean showing your slides to the people you share an office with.

682
00:42:31.590 --> 00:42:36.280
[S11] The problem with that is that if people know what you're doing, they will hallucinate

683
00:42:36.280 --> 00:42:40.809
[S11] that there's material in your presentation that isn't there.

684
00:42:40.809 --> 00:42:47.159
[S11] A variation on this theme, by the way, is your faculty supervisor is not a very good

685
00:42:47.159 --> 00:42:51.630
[S11] person to help you debug a talk, because they, in fact, know what you're doing, and they

686
00:42:51.630 --> 00:42:56.550
[S11] will, in fact, hallucinate there's material in your presentation that isn't there.

687
00:42:56.550 --> 00:43:00.179
[S11] So you need to get together some friends who don't know what you're doing and have

688
00:43:00.179 --> 00:43:07.489
[S11] them, well, you start the practice session by saying, "If you can't make me cry, I won't

689
00:43:07.489 --> 00:43:10.239
[S11] value you as a friend anymore."

690
00:43:10.239 --> 00:43:16.460
[S11] And then when you get to the faculty on a oral exam, it will be easy.

691
00:43:16.460 --> 00:43:23.239
[S11] You see, difficulty, the amount of flak you'll get from somebody is proportional to age.

692
00:43:23.239 --> 00:43:28.849
[S11] The older somebody is, the more they understand where they are in the world.

693
00:43:28.849 --> 00:43:32.380
[S11] But the young people are trying to show the old people how smart they are, so they'll

694
00:43:32.380 --> 00:43:33.630
[S11] be vicious.

695
00:43:33.630 --> 00:43:37.300
[S11] So whenever you have an opportunity to have an examining committee that's full of people

696
00:43:37.300 --> 00:43:40.099
[S11] with gray hair, that's what you want.

697
00:43:40.099 --> 00:43:43.739
[S11] Well, that's just a word or two about something I haven't listened here.

698
00:43:43.739 --> 00:43:46.849
[S11] Let's get into the subject of job talks.

699
00:43:46.849 --> 00:43:55.320
[S11] So I was sitting in a bar many years ago in San Diego.

700
00:43:55.320 --> 00:44:01.070
[S11] I was a member of the Navy Science Board, and I was saying with a couple of my colleagues

701
00:44:01.070 --> 00:44:07.380
[S11] on the board, Dolores Eder from the University of Colorado, she made me so jealous I could

702
00:44:07.380 --> 00:44:11.849
[S11] spit, because she had written 21 books and I'd only written 17.

703
00:44:11.849 --> 00:44:19.429
[S11] And then the other one was Bill Weldon from the University of Texas.

704
00:44:19.429 --> 00:44:25.909
[S11] He was an electromagnetism guy, and he knew how to use railguns to drive steel rods through

705
00:44:25.909 --> 00:44:26.909
[S11] tank armor.

706
00:44:26.909 --> 00:44:28.739
[S11] These were interesting people.

707
00:44:28.739 --> 00:44:35.139
[S11] So I said, "What do you look for in a faculty candidate?"

708
00:44:35.139 --> 00:44:42.030
[S11] And within one microsecond, Dolores said, "They have to show us they've got some kind

709
00:44:42.030 --> 00:44:44.059
[S11] of vision."

710
00:44:44.059 --> 00:44:56.309
[S11] Quickly followed by Bill, who said, "They have to show us that they've done something."

711
00:44:56.309 --> 00:44:59.780
[S11] "Oh, that sounds good," I said.

712
00:44:59.780 --> 00:45:05.139
[S11] And then I said to them, "How long does a candidate have to establish these two things?

713
00:45:05.139 --> 00:45:06.449
[S11] What do you think?"

714
00:45:08.190 --> 00:45:11.130
[S12] Well, compare your answer to theirs.

715
00:45:15.170 --> 00:45:17.170
[S12] Five minutes.

716
00:45:17.980 --> 00:45:20.480
[S12] So if you haven't expressed your vision,

717
00:45:20.510 --> 00:45:23.599
[S12] if you haven't told people that you've done something,

718
00:45:23.630 --> 00:45:27.050
[S12] in five minutes, you've already lost.

719
00:45:27.079 --> 00:45:28.730
[S12] So you have to be able to do that.

720
00:45:28.760 --> 00:45:32.400
[S12] And let me just mention a couple of things in that connection.

721
00:45:32.429 --> 00:45:34.739
[S12] Here, which is, you know, the vision is in part

722
00:45:34.769 --> 00:45:39.289
[S12] a problem that somebody cares about

723
00:45:39.320 --> 00:45:43.179
[S12] and something new in your approach.

724
00:45:47.429 --> 00:45:49.699
[S12] So the problem is understanding the nature

725
00:45:49.730 --> 00:45:51.579
[S12] of human intelligence.

726
00:45:51.610 --> 00:45:53.789
[S12] And the approach is asking questions

727
00:45:53.820 --> 00:45:56.099
[S12] about what makes us different

728
00:45:56.130 --> 00:45:59.260
[S12] from chimpanzees and Neanderthals.

729
00:45:59.289 --> 00:46:00.860
[S12] Is it merely a matter of quantity?

730
00:46:00.889 --> 00:46:02.440
[S12] Or are we just a little bit smarter

731
00:46:02.469 --> 00:46:04.449
[S12] in some continuous way?

732
00:46:04.480 --> 00:46:07.679
[S12] Or do we have something that's fundamentally different

733
00:46:07.710 --> 00:46:11.659
[S12] that chimpanzees don't have and Neanderthals either?

734
00:46:11.690 --> 00:46:13.940
[S12] And the answer is, yes.

735
00:46:13.969 --> 00:46:15.769
[S12] We do have something different.

736
00:46:15.800 --> 00:46:18.210
[S12] We are symbolic creatures.

737
00:46:18.239 --> 00:46:20.659
[S12] And because we're symbolic creatures,

738
00:46:20.690 --> 00:46:23.630
[S12] we can, um,

739
00:46:23.659 --> 00:46:25.699
[S12] we can build symbolic descriptions

740
00:46:25.730 --> 00:46:27.800
[S12] of relations and events.

741
00:46:27.829 --> 00:46:30.300
[S12] We can string them together and make stories.

742
00:46:30.329 --> 00:46:31.440
[S12] And because we can make stories,

743
00:46:31.469 --> 00:46:32.900
[S12] that's what makes us different.

744
00:46:32.929 --> 00:46:35.340
[S12] So that's my stump speech.

745
00:46:35.369 --> 00:46:37.699
[S12] That's how I start most of my talks

746
00:46:37.730 --> 00:46:40.340
[S12] on my own personal research.

747
00:46:40.369 --> 00:46:43.639
[S12] How do you express the notion

748
00:46:43.670 --> 00:46:46.170
[S12] that you've done something?

749
00:46:46.199 --> 00:46:50.219
[S12] By listing the steps that need to be taken

750
00:46:50.250 --> 00:46:53.829
[S12] in order to achieve the solution of that problem.

751
00:46:53.860 --> 00:46:55.699
[S12] You don't have to have done all of those steps,

752
00:46:55.730 --> 00:46:59.480
[S12] but you can say, here's what needs to be done.

753
00:46:59.510 --> 00:47:00.570
[S12] An example.

754
00:47:00.599 --> 00:47:01.699
[S12] Here's what needs to be done.

755
00:47:01.730 --> 00:47:04.400
[S12] We need to specify some behavior.

756
00:47:04.429 --> 00:47:06.900
[S12] We need to enumerate the constraints

757
00:47:06.929 --> 00:47:09.820
[S12] that make it possible to deal with that behavior.

758
00:47:09.849 --> 00:47:11.199
[S12] We have to implement a system

759
00:47:11.230 --> 00:47:12.199
[S12] because we're engineers

760
00:47:12.230 --> 00:47:14.340
[S12] and we don't think that we've understood something

761
00:47:14.369 --> 00:47:16.400
[S12] unless we can build it.

762
00:47:16.429 --> 00:47:17.860
[S12] And we've built such a system

763
00:47:17.889 --> 00:47:19.539
[S12] and we're about to demonstrate it to you today.

764
00:47:19.570 --> 00:47:20.860
[S12] That would be an example

765
00:47:20.889 --> 00:47:23.199
[S12] of enumerating a series of steps

766
00:47:23.230 --> 00:47:26.449
[S12] needed to realize the vision.

767
00:47:26.480 --> 00:47:30.289
[S12] So then, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah.

768
00:47:30.329 --> 00:47:32.789
[S12] And then you conclude by,

769
00:47:32.829 --> 00:47:37.429
[S12] you conclude by enumerating your contributions.

770
00:47:42.260 --> 00:47:45.099
[S12] It's a kind of mirror of these steps

771
00:47:45.130 --> 00:47:47.659
[S12] and helps to establish that you've done something.

772
00:47:47.690 --> 00:47:49.940
[S12] So that's a kind of general purpose framework

773
00:47:49.969 --> 00:47:53.289
[S12] for doing a technical talk.

774
00:47:53.329 --> 00:47:58.099
[S12] Now, only a few more things left to do today.

775
00:47:58.130 --> 00:48:00.500
[S12] Getting famous is the next item on our agenda

776
00:48:00.530 --> 00:48:02.260
[S12] because once you've got the job,

777
00:48:02.289 --> 00:48:03.199
[S12] you need to think a little bit

778
00:48:03.230 --> 00:48:04.860
[S12] about how you're going to be recognized

779
00:48:04.889 --> 00:48:06.829
[S12] for what you do.

780
00:48:06.860 --> 00:48:08.860
[S12] So.

781
00:48:14.760 --> 00:48:15.539
[S12] Well, first of all,

782
00:48:15.570 --> 00:48:19.260
[S12] why should you care about getting famous?

783
00:48:19.289 --> 00:48:21.539
[S12] I thought about this in connection

784
00:48:21.570 --> 00:48:29.429
[S12] with a fundraising event I attended once.

785
00:48:29.460 --> 00:48:31.099
[S12] Fundraising event for raising money

786
00:48:31.130 --> 00:48:32.940
[S12] to save Venice from going underwater

787
00:48:32.969 --> 00:48:34.940
[S12] and having all of its art destroyed.

788
00:48:34.969 --> 00:48:38.340
[S12] Anyway, I was sitting here

789
00:48:38.369 --> 00:48:41.340
[S12] and J.C. was sitting here.

790
00:48:41.369 --> 00:48:46.130
[S12] That was Julia, the late Julia Child.

791
00:48:49.329 --> 00:48:51.139
[S12] And as the evening wore on,

792
00:48:51.170 --> 00:48:53.059
[S12] more and more people would come up

793
00:48:53.090 --> 00:48:55.940
[S12] and ask Julia to autograph something

794
00:48:55.969 --> 00:49:01.260
[S12] or express a feeling that she had changed their life.

795
00:49:01.289 --> 00:49:04.539
[S12] It just happened over and over again.

796
00:49:04.570 --> 00:49:07.699
[S12] So eventually, I turned to Julia and I said,

797
00:49:07.730 --> 00:49:11.539
[S12] "Ms. Child, is it fun to be famous?"

798
00:49:11.570 --> 00:49:12.940
[S12] And she thought about it for a second

799
00:49:12.969 --> 00:49:17.059
[S12] and she said, "You get used to it."

800
00:49:17.090 --> 00:49:19.219
[S12] But you know what, heard to me?

801
00:49:19.250 --> 00:49:22.630
[S12] You never get used to being ignored.

802
00:49:22.659 --> 00:49:24.980
[S12] So it's, you know, it's,

803
00:49:25.010 --> 00:49:26.380
[S12] here's a way to think about it.

804
00:49:26.400 --> 00:49:28.659
[S12] Your ideas are like your children.

805
00:49:28.690 --> 00:49:31.460
[S12] And you don't want them to go into the world in rags.

806
00:49:31.489 --> 00:49:32.500
[S12] So what you want to do

807
00:50:07.289 --> 00:50:13.460
[S13] One of the things you need to do is to make sure that you have some kind of symbol

808
00:50:13.460 --> 00:50:15.860
[S13] associated with your work.

809
00:50:15.860 --> 00:50:21.150
[S13] So this arch example is actually from my PhD thesis many, many years ago.

810
00:50:21.150 --> 00:50:28.550
[S13] And in the course of my work at that time, this work on arch learning became mildly

811
00:50:28.550 --> 00:50:30.969
[S13] famous, and I didn't know why.

812
00:50:30.969 --> 00:50:35.420
[S13] It was only many years later that I realized that that work accidentally had all of the

813
00:50:35.420 --> 00:50:37.440
[S13] elements on this star.

814
00:50:37.440 --> 00:50:41.070
[S13] So the first element is that there was a kind of symbol.

815
00:50:41.070 --> 00:50:46.789
[S13] It's the arch itself.

816
00:50:46.789 --> 00:50:49.920
[S13] Next thing you need is some kind of slogan.

817
00:50:49.920 --> 00:50:54.579
[S13] A kind of phrase that provides a handle on the work.

818
00:50:54.579 --> 00:51:01.489
[S13] And in this case, the phrase was "one-shot learning."

819
00:51:01.489 --> 00:51:07.079
[S13] And it was "one-shot" because the program I wrote learned something definite from every

820
00:51:07.079 --> 00:51:10.170
[S13] example that was presented to us.

821
00:51:10.170 --> 00:51:15.780
[S13] So in going from a model based on this configuration to something that isn't an arch based on

822
00:51:15.780 --> 00:51:19.869
[S13] that configuration, the program learned that it has to be on top.

823
00:51:19.869 --> 00:51:22.780
[S13] One-shot learning.

824
00:51:22.780 --> 00:51:26.869
[S13] So that's a simple slogan.

825
00:51:26.869 --> 00:51:29.639
[S13] And now we need a surprise.

826
00:51:29.639 --> 00:51:37.099
[S13] Yeah, the surprise is you don't need a million examples of something to learn.

827
00:51:37.099 --> 00:51:43.389
[S13] You can do it with one example if you're smart enough to make use of that example appropriately.

828
00:51:43.389 --> 00:51:44.449
[S13] So that was the surprise.

829
00:51:44.449 --> 00:51:48.449
[S13] You can learn something definite from each example.

830
00:51:48.449 --> 00:51:54.559
[S13] Next item was a salient idea.

831
00:51:54.559 --> 00:51:58.019
[S13] Now, when I say "salient idea," I don't mean important.

832
00:51:58.019 --> 00:52:02.639
[S13] What I mean is an idea that sticks out.

833
00:52:02.639 --> 00:52:06.099
[S13] Some theses, funnily enough, have too many good ideas, and you don't know what it's

834
00:52:06.099 --> 00:52:08.139
[S13] all about because which one is it?

835
00:52:08.139 --> 00:52:10.480
[S13] So you need an idea that sticks out.

836
00:52:10.480 --> 00:52:20.539
[S13] And the idea that stuck out here was a notion of a near miss.

837
00:52:20.539 --> 00:52:27.190
[S13] You see, this is not an arch, but it doesn't miss by much, so it's a near miss.

838
00:52:27.190 --> 00:52:37.340
[S13] And finally, you need to tell the story of how you did it, how it works, why it's important.

839
00:52:37.340 --> 00:52:44.039
[S13] So that's a bit on how to, not so much get famous, but how to ensure that your work

840
00:52:44.039 --> 00:52:45.369
[S13] is recognized.

841
00:52:45.369 --> 00:52:52.570
[S13] Well, we're almost finished, because now we're down to this last item, which is how

842
00:52:52.570 --> 00:52:53.570
[S13] to stop.

843
00:52:53.570 --> 00:53:03.730
[S13] And when we come to that, there's a question of, all right, well, what is the final slide?

844
00:53:03.730 --> 00:53:11.900
[S13] And what are the final words?

845
00:53:11.900 --> 00:53:15.980
[S13] So for the final slide, let me give you some examples of possibilities.

846
00:53:15.980 --> 00:53:23.369
[S13] How about this one?

847
00:53:23.369 --> 00:53:35.010
[S13] Well, you might see that slide and think to yourself, there are 1,000 faculty at MIT,

848
00:53:35.010 --> 00:53:37.010
[S13] nice piece of work, but not so much.

849
00:53:37.010 --> 00:53:42.340
[S13] But it's only a tiny piece of work if you invite by 1,000.

850
00:53:42.340 --> 00:53:45.869
[S13] So you show a whole gigantic list of collaborators at the end of a talk.

851
00:53:45.869 --> 00:53:51.510
[S13] It's a kind of letdown, because it suggests that nobody knows, well, did you do anything

852
00:53:51.510 --> 00:53:52.510
[S13] significant?

853
00:53:52.510 --> 00:53:56.480
[S13] Now, you've got to recognize your collaborators, right?

854
00:53:56.480 --> 00:53:58.400
[S13] So where do you do that?

855
00:53:58.400 --> 00:53:59.650
[S13] Not on the last slide.

856
00:53:59.650 --> 00:54:01.260
[S13] On the first slide.

857
00:54:01.260 --> 00:54:02.480
[S13] All this was on the first slide.

858
00:54:02.480 --> 00:54:04.480
[S13] These are the collaborators.

859
00:54:04.480 --> 00:54:05.650
[S13] So you don't want to put them at the end.

860
00:54:05.650 --> 00:54:09.460
[S13] You don't want a slide like this.

861
00:54:09.460 --> 00:54:12.289
[S13] How about this one?

862
00:54:12.289 --> 00:54:16.039
[S13] This is the worst possible way to end a talk.

863
00:54:16.039 --> 00:54:20.650
[S13] Because this slide can be up there for 20 minutes.

864
00:54:20.650 --> 00:54:22.039
[S13] I've seen it happen.

865
00:54:22.039 --> 00:54:25.280
[S13] It squanders real estate.

866
00:54:25.280 --> 00:54:28.599
[S13] It squanders an opportunity to tell people who you are.

867
00:54:28.599 --> 00:54:33.110
[S13] It's just...

868
00:54:33.110 --> 00:54:37.980
[S13] What about this one?

869
00:54:37.980 --> 00:54:38.980
[S13] I often see it.

870
00:54:38.980 --> 00:54:42.480
[S13] I've never seen anybody write it down.

871
00:54:42.480 --> 00:54:44.800
[S13] Also, it wastes opportunity.

872
00:54:44.800 --> 00:54:52.050
[S13] Oh, my God.

873
00:54:52.050 --> 00:54:56.250
[S13] Even worse.

874
00:54:56.250 --> 00:54:59.469
[S13] All of these slides do nothing for you.

875
00:54:59.469 --> 00:55:05.050
[S13] They waste an opportunity for you to tell people, to leave people with what you, with

876
00:55:05.050 --> 00:55:05.719
[S13] who you are.

877
00:55:07.510 --> 00:55:09.280
[S14] Well, what about this?

878
00:55:10.659 --> 00:55:12.630
[S14] Is this a good one?

879
00:55:12.659 --> 00:55:16.280
[S14] It might seem so at first, but here's the problem.

880
00:55:19.510 --> 00:55:22.230
[S14] If you say, "These are my conclusions,"

881
00:55:22.260 --> 00:55:26.090
[S14] these are perfectly legitimate conclusions that nobody cares about.

882
00:55:26.119 --> 00:55:28.849
[S14] What they care about is what you have done.

883
00:55:28.880 --> 00:55:31.380
[S14] And that's why your final slide should have this label.

884
00:55:32.690 --> 00:55:34.619
[S14] Contributions.

885
00:55:34.650 --> 00:55:39.039
[S14] It's a mirror of what I said over there about how job talks ought to be like a sandwich.

886
00:55:39.070 --> 00:55:43.250
[S14] And the final slide, the one that's up there while people are asking questions and filing out,

887
00:55:43.280 --> 00:55:46.570
[S14] it ought to be the one that has your contributions on it.

888
00:55:46.599 --> 00:55:50.329
[S14] Here's an example from my own stump speech.

889
00:55:50.360 --> 00:55:54.210
[S14] Yeah, this is what I talk about a lot.

890
00:55:54.239 --> 00:55:57.880
[S14] Yes, here are the things that I typically demonstrate.

891
00:56:02.349 --> 00:56:04.610
[S14] And I wait for people to read it.

892
00:56:04.639 --> 00:56:08.190
[S14] And then the final element there is, this is what we get out of it.

893
00:56:10.000 --> 00:56:12.500
[S14] So that's an example of a contribution slide.

894
00:56:13.960 --> 00:56:18.659
[S14] All right, now, what about the other part?

895
00:56:18.699 --> 00:56:22.329
[S14] You know, you've got your final slide up there as a contribution slide.

896
00:56:22.360 --> 00:56:25.519
[S14] Somehow, you have to tell people you're finished.

897
00:56:25.550 --> 00:56:28.579
[S14] So let's check out a few possibilities.

898
00:56:31.349 --> 00:56:37.420
[S14] One thing you could do in the final words is you could tell a joke.

899
00:56:41.030 --> 00:56:41.889
[S14] It's okay.

900
00:56:43.730 --> 00:56:46.989
[S14] By the time you're done, people have adjusted themselves to your voice parameters.

901
00:56:47.019 --> 00:56:48.190
[S14] They're ready for a joke.

902
00:56:51.139 --> 00:56:54.349
[S14] I was sitting in another bar this time in Austin, Texas,

903
00:56:54.380 --> 00:56:57.489
[S14] with a colleague of mine named Doug Lanat.

904
00:56:57.519 --> 00:57:00.050
[S14] And Doug's a fantastic speaker.

905
00:57:00.079 --> 00:57:04.389
[S14] And so I said to Doug, "Doug, you're a fantastic speaker.

906
00:57:04.420 --> 00:57:06.010
[S14] What's your secret?"

907
00:57:06.039 --> 00:57:10.610
[S14] And he said, "Oh, I always finish with a joke,

908
00:57:10.639 --> 00:57:13.090
[S14] and that way, people think they've had fun the whole time."

909
00:57:16.699 --> 00:57:19.289
[S14] So, yeah, a joke will work down there.

910
00:57:20.480 --> 00:57:21.949
[S14] How about this one?

911
00:57:32.010 --> 00:57:32.679
[S14] Thank you.

912
00:57:35.199 --> 00:57:36.179
[S14] I don't recommend it.

913
00:57:37.699 --> 00:57:39.570
[S14] It's a weak move.

914
00:57:39.599 --> 00:57:42.849
[S14] You will not go to hell if you conclude your talk by saying, "Thank you,"

915
00:57:42.880 --> 00:57:44.750
[S14] but it's a weak move, and here's why.

916
00:57:45.949 --> 00:57:49.639
[S14] When you say, "Thank you," even worse, "Thank you for listening,"

917
00:57:49.670 --> 00:57:53.769
[S14] it suggests that everybody has stayed that long out of politeness

918
00:57:53.800 --> 00:57:56.849
[S14] and that they had a profound desire to be somewhere else,

919
00:57:56.880 --> 00:57:58.570
[S14] but they're so polite, they stuck it out.

920
00:57:58.599 --> 00:57:59.980
[S14] And that's what you're thanking them for.

921
00:58:01.639 --> 00:58:04.570
[S14] So, once wild applause has started, you can mouth a "thank you,"

922
00:58:04.599 --> 00:58:06.530
[S14] and there's nothing wrong with that.

923
00:58:06.559 --> 00:58:09.380
[S14] But the last thing you do should not be saying, "Thank you."

924
00:58:11.349 --> 00:58:14.760
[S14] Not if you say to me, "Well, doesn't everybody say, 'Thank you'?"

925
00:58:14.789 --> 00:58:18.960
[S14] Well, what everybody does is not necessarily the right thing.

926
00:58:18.989 --> 00:58:22.960
[S14] And I like to illustrate how some talks can end without saying, "Thank you."

927
00:58:22.989 --> 00:58:26.369
[S14] I like to draw from political speeches,

928
00:58:26.400 --> 00:58:28.849
[S14] but the ones that I've heard recently aren't so good, so...

929
00:58:31.440 --> 00:58:35.380
[S14] So, I'm gonna have to go back a little bit.

930
00:58:36.639 --> 00:58:38.250
[S14] So here's Governor Christie.

931
00:58:38.280 --> 00:58:44.719
[S14] He gave the Republican keynote address one year.

932
00:58:44.750 --> 00:58:46.250
[S14] This is the end of his talk.

933
00:58:46.280 --> 00:58:47.289
[S14] Let's see what he does.

934
00:58:50.440 --> 00:58:53.090
[S15] And together, everybody, together,

935
00:58:53.119 --> 00:58:57.250
[S15] we will stand up once again for American greatness

936
00:58:57.280 --> 00:58:59.409
[S15] for our children and grandchildren.

937
00:58:59.440 --> 00:59:01.409
[S15] God bless you and God bless America.

938
00:59:04.750 --> 00:59:08.409
[S14] That's a classic benediction ending.

939
00:59:08.440 --> 00:59:10.349
[S14] "God bless you, God bless America."

940
00:59:11.840 --> 00:59:13.750
[S14] Now, I don't want to be partisan about this,

941
00:59:13.780 --> 00:59:18.949
[S14] so I think I better switch to the keynote address in the Democratic convention.

942
00:59:20.349 --> 00:59:22.949
[S14] That was delivered that year by Bill Clinton,

943
00:59:22.980 --> 00:59:24.579
[S14] who knows something about how to speak.

944
00:59:27.550 --> 00:59:30.010
[S15] If that is what you want,

945
00:59:30.039 --> 00:59:32.400
[S15] if that is what you believe,

946
00:59:32.429 --> 00:59:38.210
[S15] you must vote and you must reelect President Barack Obama.

947
00:59:38.239 --> 00:59:40.750
[S15] God bless you and God bless America.

948
00:59:51.010 --> 00:59:51.949
[S14] Now, watch this.

949
00:59:52.599 --> 00:59:54.630
[S16] Let's go back a little bit and redo it.

950
00:59:54.659 --> 00:59:56.309
[S16] What I want you to see is that at one point,

951
00:59:56.340 --> 00:59:59.199
[S16] he seems to be almost pressing his lips together,

952
00:59:59.230 --> 01:00:01.510
[S16] forcing himself not to say "thank you."

953
01:00:01.539 --> 01:00:03.840
[S16] Then there's another place where he does a little salute.

954
01:00:03.869 --> 01:00:05.329
[S16] So watch for those this time around.

955
01:00:06.769 --> 01:00:09.239
[S17] If that is what you want,

956
01:00:09.269 --> 01:00:11.639
[S17] if that is what you believe,

957
01:00:11.670 --> 01:00:17.440
[S17] you must vote and you must reelect President Barack Obama.

958
01:00:17.469 --> 01:00:21.170
[S17] God bless you and God bless America.

959
01:00:21.199 --> 01:00:24.469
[S16] That's where he's pursing his lips.

960
01:00:24.500 --> 01:00:25.440
[S16] There's a salute.

961
01:00:30.929 --> 01:00:31.840
[S16] Yeah, I think that's pretty good.

962
01:00:31.860 --> 01:00:33.730
[S16] Now, what are we gonna take away from this?

963
01:00:33.760 --> 01:00:38.539
[S16] Well, I suppose I could conclude this talk by saying,

964
01:00:38.570 --> 01:00:43.300
[S16] "God bless you and God bless the Massachusetts Institute of Technology."

965
01:00:43.329 --> 01:00:45.969
[S16] But it might not work so well.

966
01:00:46.000 --> 01:00:49.139
[S16] But what you can get out of this is you don't have to say "thank you."

967
01:00:49.170 --> 01:00:51.039
[S16] There are other things you can do.

968
01:00:51.070 --> 01:00:53.239
[S16] And you know, it's interesting that, over time,

969
01:00:53.269 --> 01:00:56.880
[S16] people figure this out and there's some stock ways of ending things.

970
01:00:56.909 --> 01:01:02.300
[S16] So, in the Catholic Church, in a good old Latin mass,

971
01:01:02.329 --> 01:01:04.130
[S16] it ended with "It's a...

972
01:01:04.159 --> 01:01:05.840
[S16] ...it's a...

973
01:01:05.869 --> 01:01:08.800
[S16] ...est."

974
01:01:08.829 --> 01:01:11.199
[S16] Which translates approximately to,

975
01:01:11.230 --> 01:01:15.570
[S16] "Okay, the mass is over. You can go home now."

976
01:01:15.599 --> 01:01:18.440
[S16] And of course, at musical concerts,

977
01:01:18.469 --> 01:01:21.670
[S16] you know that it's time to clap,

978
01:01:21.699 --> 01:01:23.400
[S16] not at the end of the song,

979
01:01:23.429 --> 01:01:25.929
[S16] but rather when the conductor goes over

980
01:01:25.960 --> 01:01:28.440
[S16] and shakes hands with the concert master.

981
01:01:28.469 --> 01:01:34.530
[S16] Those are conventions that tell you that the event is over.

982
01:01:34.559 --> 01:01:39.639
[S16] So, those are all possibilities for here.

983
01:01:39.670 --> 01:01:46.570
[S16] But one more possibility, and that is that you can

984
01:01:46.599 --> 01:01:48.639
[S16] salute the audience.

985
01:01:48.670 --> 01:01:51.699
[S16] And by that I mean you can say something about

986
01:01:51.730 --> 01:01:54.300
[S16] how much you value your time at a place.

987
01:01:54.329 --> 01:02:01.070
[S16] So I could say, "Well, it's been a great fun being here.

988
01:02:01.099 --> 01:02:05.300
[S16] It's been fascinating to see what you folks are doing here at MIT.

989
01:02:05.329 --> 01:02:09.000
[S16] I've been much stimulated and provoked

990
01:02:09.030 --> 01:02:10.440
[S16] by the kinds of questions you've been asked.

991
01:02:10.469 --> 01:02:14.199
[S16] It's been really great and I look forward to coming back

992
01:02:14.230 --> 01:02:16.369
[S16] on many occasions in the future."

993
01:02:16.400 --> 01:02:17.929
[S16] So that's a salute to the audience.

994
01:02:17.949 --> 01:02:18.929
[S16] You can do that.

995
01:02:21.800 --> 01:02:24.369
[S16] Well, there it is.

996
01:02:24.400 --> 01:02:26.369
[S16] You know what?

997
01:02:26.400 --> 01:02:28.099
[S16] I'm glad you're here.

998
01:02:28.130 --> 01:02:29.880
[S16] And the reason is by being here,

999
01:02:29.909 --> 01:02:32.039
[S16] I think you have demonstrated

1000
01:02:32.070 --> 01:02:34.570
[S16] an understanding that how you present

1001
01:02:34.599 --> 01:02:37.639
[S16] and how you package your ideas is an important thing.

1002
01:02:37.670 --> 01:02:40.000
[S16] And I salute you for that.

1003
01:02:40.030 --> 01:02:45.329
[S16] And I suggest that you come back again and bring your friends.
